# `std/parsing/regex` — the prefix matcher behind `PRegex`

`std/parsing/combinators.ssc` runs a `PRegex(pat)` node by asking for the longest prefix of the
input that `pat` matches. That question used to be answered by the **host**: `java.util.regex`,
reached through a string method the interpreter implemented natively. This module answers it in
ScalaScript instead.

**Why it is not a host primitive.** A host implementation has to be written once per lane —
executor, bridge, self-hosted, and each backend — and the four have to agree byte for byte, which
is what invariant I-3 requires. Regex dialects are exactly where that agreement is expensive:
Java, JavaScript and Rust disagree about what `$` means, about what `.` excludes, and about
character-class corners. A `.ssc` module is a *program*, so every lane that can run programs runs
this one, and they agree by construction rather than by testing. It is also the only form that
exists on the self-hosted lane at all, where the "host" is ScalaScript itself.

The trade is speed: this is interpreted, per character. The pattern is re-parsed on every call —
so was `Pattern.compile`'s in the implementation this replaces, so that is not a regression, but
it is the first thing to cache if a profile ever points here.

## The supported subset, and why it is closed

`v3/regex-subset-gate.sh` enumerates every pattern literal reaching `Parser.regex` in the tree and
refuses one this module cannot parse. So the subset is not a hope — it is checked at commit time,
and anything outside it fails **loudly** here rather than quietly matching the wrong text.

| supported | meaning |
|---|---|
| `a`, `\.`, `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\f` | a literal character |
| `[a-z]`, `[^\n:]`, `[ \t]` | a character class: ranges, negation, escaped members |
| `\s \S \d \D \w \W` | the shorthand classes, inside a class or out |
| `.` | any character that is not a line terminator |
| `*`, `+`, `?` | greedy repetition of the preceding item |
| `(…)` | a group, quantifiable as a unit |
| `$` | the end of the input, **or just before a line terminator that ends it** |

Refused, by name, with the pattern in the message: alternation `|`, counted repetition `{n,m}`,
the lazy and possessive quantifiers, `(?…)` in all its forms, the `^` anchor, backreferences, and
any unknown letter escape — `\b` read as the letter `b` would be a wrong answer where an error is
the honest outcome.

Matching is the ordinary backtracking algorithm, anchored at offset 0, greedy: the same thing
`lookingAt()` does.

## Pattern nodes

```scalascript
sealed trait RxNode

// One literal character, by code.
case class RxChar(code: Int) extends RxNode

// A character class. `ranges` is a FLAT list of inclusive [lo, hi] pairs — a
// single character is a pair with lo == hi — and `neg` inverts the whole test.
// Flat rather than a list of pair objects because the test runs per input
// character and this is the hot loop.
case class RxClass(neg: Boolean, ranges: List[Int]) extends RxNode

// `.` — any character that is not a line terminator.
case object RxAnyChar extends RxNode

// `$` — succeeds, consuming nothing, at the end of the input OR just before a
// line terminator that ends it. See `rxAtEndAt` for why the second half is here.
case object RxAtEnd extends RxNode

// `(...)` — a parenthesised sequence, so a quantifier can apply to all of it.
case class RxGroup(nodes: List[Any]) extends RxNode

// A quantified item. `max` is -1 for unbounded.
case class RxRep(item: Any, min: Int, max: Int) extends RxNode

// Internal, never produced by the parser: fails when the current position is
// still `at`. The matcher inserts one after an OPTIONAL iteration so that a
// zero-width body cannot repeat forever.
case class RxNotAt(at: Int) extends RxNode
```

## Parser results

Explicit records rather than tuples: a returned `(node, next)` reads the same in both, and a named
field survives a refactor of the other.

```scalascript
case class RxSeqRes(nodes: List[Any], next: Int)
case class RxItemRes(node: Any, next: Int)
case class RxClsRes(neg: Boolean, ranges: List[Int], next: Int)
```

## Escapes

```scalascript
// The escapes that stand for a single character, as a code, or -1 for "not one
// of these". A LETTER or DIGIT that is not in the table is refused by the
// caller rather than read as itself: `\b` is a word boundary in every dialect
// this module replaces and `\1` is a backreference, so reading either as the
// bare character would be a wrong answer where an error is correct.
def rxEscChar(c: Int): Int =
  if c == 110 then 10
  else if c == 116 then 9
  else if c == 114 then 13
  else if c == 102 then 12
  else if c >= 97 && c <= 122 then -1
  else if c >= 65 && c <= 90 then -1
  else if c >= 48 && c <= 57 then -1
  else c

// The shorthand classes as a flat range list, or an empty list when `c` is not
// one of them. `\s` is Java's set: space, tab, newline, vertical tab, form
// feed, carriage return — codes 9..13 and 32.
def rxEscRanges(c: Int): List[Int] =
  if c == 115 || c == 83 then List(9, 13, 32, 32)
  else if c == 100 || c == 68 then List(48, 57)
  else if c == 119 || c == 87 then List(48, 57, 65, 90, 95, 95, 97, 122)
  else List()

// True for the negated spellings `\S \D \W`.
def rxEscNeg(c: Int): Boolean = c == 83 || c == 68 || c == 87

// The line terminators `.` excludes and `$` is allowed to sit in front of:
// newline, carriage return, next-line, line separator, paragraph separator.
def rxIsLineTerm(c: Int): Boolean =
  c == 10 || c == 13 || c == 133 || c == 8232 || c == 8233

// `$` is NOT simply "pos == length", and the difference is load-bearing: against
// an input ending in a newline, `$` matched BEFORE that newline in the
// implementation this module replaces, so defining it the obvious way would
// silently change what existing parsers accept. The rule is transcribed here on
// purpose — the point of moving the matcher into ScalaScript is that a corner
// like this is written down in ONE readable place instead of being three host
// engines' defaults, each subtly different.
def rxAtEndAt(s: String, pos: Int): Boolean =
  val n = s.length
  if pos == n then true
  else if pos == n - 1 then rxIsLineTerm(s.charAt(pos).toInt)
  else if pos == n - 2 then s.charAt(pos).toInt == 13 && s.charAt(pos + 1).toInt == 10
  else false
```

## Matching

```scalascript
// Is `c` inside the flat [lo, hi] pair list?
def rxClassHas(ranges: List[Int], c: Int): Boolean =
  var rs = ranges
  var found = false
  while !found && !rs.isEmpty do
    val lo = rs.head
    val hi = rs.tail.head
    if c >= lo && c <= hi then found = true
    rs = rs.tail.tail
  found

// The nodes that consume EXACTLY one character and can match in only one way.
// Repetition of one of these needs no backtracking search, only a scan and a
// give-back, which is the fast path in `rxMatchRep`.
def rxIsSimple(n: Any): Boolean =
  n match
    case RxChar(code) => true
    case RxAnyChar => true
    case RxClass(neg, ranges) => true
    case other => false

// Does the width-1 node `n` match the character at `p`?
def rxSimpleAt(n: Any, s: String, p: Int): Boolean =
  if p >= s.length then false
  else
    val c = s.charAt(p).toInt
    n match
      case RxChar(code) => c == code
      case RxAnyChar => !rxIsLineTerm(c)
      case RxClass(neg, ranges) => rxClassHas(ranges, c) != neg
      case other => false

// Match `ns` against `s` starting at `pos`. Returns the end offset, or -1.
// The node list IS the continuation: a group splices its body in front of the
// rest, and a repetition rewrites itself into one iteration followed by a
// smaller repetition.
def rxMatch(ns: List[Any], s: String, pos: Int): Int =
  if ns.isEmpty then pos
  else
    val n = ns.head
    val rest = ns.tail
    n match
      case RxAtEnd => if rxAtEndAt(s, pos) then rxMatch(rest, s, pos) else -1
      case RxNotAt(at) => if pos == at then -1 else rxMatch(rest, s, pos)
      case RxGroup(inner) => rxMatch(inner ++ rest, s, pos)
      case RxRep(item, min, max) => rxMatchRep(item, min, max, rest, s, pos)
      case other => if rxSimpleAt(n, s, pos) then rxMatch(rest, s, pos + 1) else -1

// Greedy repetition with backtracking.
def rxMatchRep(item: Any, min: Int, max: Int, rest: List[Any], s: String, pos: Int): Int =
  if rxIsSimple(item) then
    // Width-1 and deterministic: take as much as the bound allows, then hand
    // characters back one at a time until the rest of the pattern fits. Every
    // pattern in the tree except the two with a quantified GROUP lands here.
    var p = pos
    var n = 0
    while (max < 0 || n < max) && rxSimpleAt(item, s, p) do
      p = p + 1
      n = n + 1
    if n < min then -1
    else
      var q = p
      var res = -1
      while res < 0 && q >= pos + min do
        res = rxMatch(rest, s, q)
        if res < 0 then q = q - 1
      res
  else
    // General case: try one more iteration, then settle for none. The `RxNotAt`
    // guard stops a zero-width body from repeating forever, and it is inserted
    // ONLY once the minimum is met, because a MANDATORY iteration is allowed to
    // be zero-width — `(a*)+` against "" matches, and must keep matching here.
    var greedy = -1
    if max != 0 then
      val nextMax = if max < 0 then -1 else max - 1
      val nextMin = if min > 0 then min - 1 else 0
      val tail1 = List(RxRep(item, nextMin, nextMax)) ++ rest
      val body = if min > 0 then List(item) ++ tail1 else List(item) ++ List(RxNotAt(pos)) ++ tail1
      greedy = rxMatch(body, s, pos)
    if greedy >= 0 then greedy
    else if min == 0 then rxMatch(rest, s, pos)
    else -1
```

## Parsing a pattern

```scalascript
// Parse the body of `[...]`, starting just after the `[`.
def rxParseClass(pat: String, start: Int): RxClsRes =
  var i = start
  var neg = false
  if i < pat.length && pat.charAt(i).toInt == 94 then
    neg = true
    i = i + 1
  // Annotated rather than inferred from a seed element. `var rs = List(0); rs = rs.tail` reads as
  // an empty list on the interpreter and types as `List[Int]` on the JVM lane, which compiles this
  // file as real Scala — that lane is what caught the same shape in `rxParseSeq`, where the list
  // holds nodes and `List[Int]` is simply wrong.
  var ranges: List[Int] = List()
  var closed = false
  while !closed do
    if i >= pat.length then
      throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: unterminated character class in /" + pat + "/")
    val c = pat.charAt(i).toInt
    if c == 93 then
      closed = true
      i = i + 1
    else
      var lo = c
      i = i + 1
      if lo == 92 then
        if i >= pat.length then
          throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: trailing backslash in /" + pat + "/")
        val e = pat.charAt(i).toInt
        i = i + 1
        val sub = rxEscRanges(e)
        if !sub.isEmpty then
          if rxEscNeg(e) then
            throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: a negated shorthand inside a character class is outside the supported subset, in /" + pat + "/")
          ranges = ranges ++ sub
          // Consumed as a whole sub-class, so it is not a range endpoint.
          lo = -1
        else
          lo = rxEscChar(e)
          if lo < 0 then
            throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: unsupported escape at offset " + (i - 1) + " in /" + pat + "/")
      if lo >= 0 then
        var hi = lo
        // `a-z`. A `-` immediately before the `]` is a literal dash, not a range.
        if i + 1 < pat.length && pat.charAt(i).toInt == 45 && pat.charAt(i + 1).toInt != 93 then
          i = i + 1
          var h = pat.charAt(i).toInt
          i = i + 1
          if h == 92 then
            if i >= pat.length then
              throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: trailing backslash in /" + pat + "/")
            val e2 = pat.charAt(i).toInt
            i = i + 1
            h = rxEscChar(e2)
            if h < 0 then
              throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: unsupported escape at offset " + (i - 1) + " in /" + pat + "/")
          hi = h
        ranges = ranges ++ List(lo, hi)
  RxClsRes(neg, ranges, i)

// Parse one atom and any postfix quantifier.
def rxParseItem(pat: String, start: Int): RxItemRes =
  var i = start
  val c = pat.charAt(i).toInt
  // Overwritten by every branch below; the initial value is never observed.
  var node: Any = RxAtEnd
  if c == 40 then
    i = i + 1
    if i < pat.length && pat.charAt(i).toInt == 63 then
      throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: a (?...) group is outside the supported subset, in /" + pat + "/")
    val inner = rxParseSeq(pat, i)
    i = inner.next
    if i >= pat.length then
      throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: unclosed group in /" + pat + "/")
    i = i + 1
    node = RxGroup(inner.nodes)
  else if c == 91 then
    val cls = rxParseClass(pat, i + 1)
    i = cls.next
    node = RxClass(cls.neg, cls.ranges)
  else if c == 92 then
    i = i + 1
    if i >= pat.length then
      throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: trailing backslash in /" + pat + "/")
    val e = pat.charAt(i).toInt
    i = i + 1
    val sub = rxEscRanges(e)
    if !sub.isEmpty then node = RxClass(rxEscNeg(e), sub)
    else
      val lit = rxEscChar(e)
      if lit < 0 then
        throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: unsupported escape at offset " + (i - 1) + " in /" + pat + "/")
      node = RxChar(lit)
  else if c == 46 then
    i = i + 1
    node = RxAnyChar
  else if c == 36 then
    i = i + 1
    node = RxAtEnd
  else if c == 94 then
    throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: the ^ anchor is outside the supported subset (matching is already anchored at offset 0), in /" + pat + "/")
  else if c == 124 then
    throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: alternation | is outside the supported subset, in /" + pat + "/")
  else if c == 123 then
    throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: counted repetition {n,m} is outside the supported subset, in /" + pat + "/")
  else if c == 42 || c == 43 || c == 63 then
    throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: a quantifier at offset " + i + " has nothing to repeat, in /" + pat + "/")
  else
    i = i + 1
    node = RxChar(c)
  if i < pat.length then
    val q = pat.charAt(i).toInt
    if q == 42 || q == 43 || q == 63 then
      i = i + 1
      if i < pat.length then
        val q2 = pat.charAt(i).toInt
        if q2 == 63 || q2 == 43 then
          throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: a lazy or possessive quantifier at offset " + i + " is outside the supported subset, in /" + pat + "/")
      if q == 42 then node = RxRep(node, 0, -1)
      else if q == 43 then node = RxRep(node, 1, -1)
      else node = RxRep(node, 0, 1)
  RxItemRes(node, i)

// Parse items until `)` or the end of the pattern. The caller consumes the `)`.
def rxParseSeq(pat: String, start: Int): RxSeqRes =
  var i = start
  var acc: List[Any] = List()
  var done = false
  while !done do
    if i >= pat.length then done = true
    else if pat.charAt(i).toInt == 41 then done = true
    else
      val it = rxParseItem(pat, i)
      acc = acc ++ List(it.node)
      i = it.next
  RxSeqRes(acc, i)

// Parse a whole pattern.
def rxParse(pat: String): List[Any] =
  val r = rxParseSeq(pat, 0)
  if r.next != pat.length then
    throw RuntimeException("std.parsing.regex: unbalanced ) at offset " + r.next + " in /" + pat + "/")
  r.nodes
```

## The entry point

```scalascript
// The longest prefix of `s` that `pat` matches, or None. Anchored at offset 0
// and greedy — the contract `PRegex` was written against.
def regexMatchPrefix(s: String, pat: String): Option[String] =
  val end = rxMatch(rxParse(pat), s, 0)
  if end < 0 then None else Some(s.take(end))

extension (s: String)
  // The idiomatic spelling. `combinators.ssc` deliberately calls
  // `regexMatchPrefix` directly instead: a `.matchPrefix` METHOD call can be
  // answered by a host string method on a lane whose front does not rewrite
  // extension calls, and it would be answered SILENTLY, with a different
  // implementation of the same name. A plain call cannot be resolved that way.
  def matchPrefix(pat: String): Option[String] = regexMatchPrefix(s, pat)
```
